    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the exchange around Shift of Power line by line and unpack how the Russian works.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "igor",
      "t": "Шеф, Федю уволили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Boss, Fedya has been fired.' the speaker delivers a punchy conversational reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'to fire'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты что себе позволяешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you think you are doing?' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'to allow'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не позволю увольнять моих людей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will not let you fire my people.' Chef Barinov uses the perfective verb meaning 'will allow' to focus on the single completed result, pairing it with 'to fire' and 'people'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "igor",
      "t": "Здесь я хозяин.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am in charge here.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'here' directly with 'owner', combining 'here' with 'owner'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "С меня хватит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have had enough.' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'enough'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "igor",
      "t": "Вы все уволены.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are all fired.' The character uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'fired', keeping a formal or respectful register. Then Elena responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Я так не думаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I do not think so.' Elena delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'I think'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Я являюсь владелицей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am the owner.' Elena chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'be' for the ongoing action, followed by 'owner'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "igor",
      "t": "Этого не может быть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That cannot be.' the speaker chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'can' for the ongoing action, followed by 'be'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
